WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.133 --> 00:00:01.768
In October 2017, a

00:00:01.768 --> 00:00:03.303
SWAT team showed up at Jameson

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.038
Lopp’s house in North Carolina,

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:05.638
allegedly

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:06.840
because of a fake complaint

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:07.574
by a person

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:09.242
angered by one of his tweets.

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:10.710
So he posted a video of himself

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.178
firing an AR-15

00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:13.413
and then embarked on a journey

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:15.215
to disappear in the physical world,

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:16.683
unreachable by his enemies,

00:00:16.683 --> 00:00:18.218
and far from the prying eyes

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:19.452
of the surveillance state.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.521
Lopp had been obsessed with privacy

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:22.889
long before the SWAT-ing.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:23.690
He's visually a

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.058
throwback to the long bearded

00:00:25.058 --> 00:00:26.826
mathematicians and cipher punks

00:00:26.826 --> 00:00:27.827
who in the nineties

00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:29.396
believed that recent breakthroughs

00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:30.397
in cryptography

00:00:30.397 --> 00:00:31.431
could enable levels

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.166
of personal freedom and privacy

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:35.402
beyond anyone's wildest dreams.

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:36.503
Many of the ideas

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:37.771
and technical innovations

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.072
that came out of the Cypherpunk

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:40.607
movement were eventually folded

00:00:40.607 --> 00:00:41.574
into Bitcoin.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:41.875
Government

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:42.976
is not a monolithic thing,

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.444
which is your enemy.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:45.545
It's a multitudinous thing

00:00:45.545 --> 00:00:46.846
which is your enemy.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.114
Lopp even calls himself

00:00:48.114 --> 00:00:49.449
a professional cypherpunk.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.151
Carrying on the movement's legacy,

00:00:51.151 --> 00:00:53.520
the company Lopp co-founded, Casa,

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:54.721
is trying to make it easier

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:55.989
for people to hold custody

00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:57.190
of their own Bitcoin.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.591
In keeping with the Cypherpunk

00:00:58.591 --> 00:00:59.526
ethos,

00:00:59.526 --> 00:01:00.794
instead of storing their money

00:01:00.794 --> 00:01:02.462
on third party exchanges

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:03.963
where regulators can impose

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.365
arbitrary rules.

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:06.833
After the SWAT raid, Lopp

00:01:06.833 --> 00:01:08.334
changed his phone number, set up

00:01:08.334 --> 00:01:10.070
LLCs to hide his true

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:11.304
name and address,

00:01:11.304 --> 00:01:12.939
encrypted his communications

00:01:12.939 --> 00:01:14.841
and even bought a decoy house

00:01:14.841 --> 00:01:15.375
to serve

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:16.843
as a physical mailing address,

00:01:16.843 --> 00:01:17.610
which he needed

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:19.412
to satisfy the DMV's requirement

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.680
for a driver's license.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:21.815
To check his work,

00:01:21.815 --> 00:01:23.316
he hired private investigators

00:01:23.316 --> 00:01:24.117
to tail him.

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:25.985
We the cipher punks are dedicated

00:01:25.985 --> 00:01:26.553
to building

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:27.720
anonymous systems,

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:28.655
wrote Eric Hughes

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:30.557
in his 1993 manifesto,

00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:32.292
and we don't much care

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:33.026
if you don't approve

00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:34.327
of the software we write.

00:01:34.327 --> 00:01:36.196
Jameson Lopp is an enigmatic

00:01:36.196 --> 00:01:37.430
privacy obsessive,

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.032
fighting to keep that dream alive.

00:01:45.205 --> 00:01:46.039
Why should I

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.640
self-custody my Bitcoin?

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:47.974
Well,

00:01:47.974 --> 00:01:49.876
it really comes down to what

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:52.345
security model you want

00:01:52.345 --> 00:01:54.781
and why Bitcoin was created

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:55.682
in the first place.

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:57.083
I mean, you read the whitepaper,

00:01:57.083 --> 00:01:59.452
you read what Satoshi said.

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:00.153
Trusted third

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:02.789
parties are security holes.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:04.591
This is,

00:02:04.591 --> 00:02:06.793
this is a fundamental issue

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:08.061
that we've been dealing with,

00:02:08.061 --> 00:02:09.162
you know, from inception.

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:09.929
When we talk about

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:12.632
being your own bank.

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:13.633
It's, it's

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:14.601
something that Satoshi

00:02:14.601 --> 00:02:18.037
sought to make possible, but

00:02:18.304 --> 00:02:21.307
from a feasible perspective,

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:22.108
most people don't

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.343
want to be their own bank.

00:02:23.343 --> 00:02:24.611
There's there's a reason

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:25.545
why banks exist.

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:26.613
People don't want to

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.515
have to deal with

00:02:28.515 --> 00:02:29.816
all of the hassles that come

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.284
with securing

00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:32.952
highly valuable things.

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:35.221
But what we're really saying

00:02:35.221 --> 00:02:35.455
is, like,

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:39.626
if you want to have a level of bank

00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:40.326
security,

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:42.662
if you want to be able to operate

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:43.329
in such a way

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:43.930
that you don't have

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.031
to ask permission

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:46.499
from a third party,

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:47.901
then you do have to take

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:49.435
on some responsibility.

00:02:49.435 --> 00:02:49.836
And, you know,

00:02:49.836 --> 00:02:50.737
this is a problem

00:02:50.737 --> 00:02:52.105
that I've been working on

00:02:52.105 --> 00:02:53.373
for eight years now,

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.175
and we're still a long ways away

00:02:55.175 --> 00:02:56.376
from having it solved.

00:02:56.376 --> 00:02:57.410
But I do believe, you know,

00:02:57.410 --> 00:03:00.013
we keep making incremental progress

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:02.782
towards decreasing

00:03:02.782 --> 00:03:03.983
the amount of effort

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:04.784
that is required

00:03:04.784 --> 00:03:06.386
for someone to be their own bank.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:07.220
What does that look like,

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:07.921
decreasing the amount

00:03:07.921 --> 00:03:08.888
of effort required?

00:03:08.888 --> 00:03:09.789
It involves

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:12.692
taking all of these best practices

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:13.593
and all of these things

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.727
that we have learned over

00:03:14.727 --> 00:03:16.196
the years,

00:03:16.196 --> 00:03:17.530
really learning from other people's

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.332
mistakes and catastrophes

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:20.133
and packing,

00:03:20.133 --> 00:03:22.602
packaging that up into user

00:03:22.602 --> 00:03:23.803
friendly software

00:03:23.803 --> 00:03:25.505
so that the user doesn't

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:26.873
have to read

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:28.775
novels and novels of documents

00:03:28.775 --> 00:03:30.510
about what the best practices are.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:30.977
Rather,

00:03:30.977 --> 00:03:32.912
they just follow the instructions

00:03:32.912 --> 00:03:33.546
of what the software

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:34.814
is telling them to do,

00:03:34.814 --> 00:03:36.616
and that puts them into a position

00:03:36.616 --> 00:03:37.650
where they are

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.918
automatically following

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:39.919
the best practices.

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.221
Well, so what does that look like

00:03:41.221 --> 00:03:42.288
a little bit more concretely?

00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:42.755
Like, say,

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:43.756
I personally am

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:45.391
trying to start on the journey

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:46.326
specifically

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:48.261
or what are two or three steps

00:03:48.261 --> 00:03:49.896
I could take to begin

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:53.666
to take control of my own privacy

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:56.135
to a greater extent and stop using,

00:03:56.135 --> 00:03:57.370
you know, Coinbase.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:57.604
Yeah.

00:03:57.604 --> 00:03:59.906
So I look at it in terms of

00:03:59.906 --> 00:04:01.541
what do you have to lose

00:04:01.541 --> 00:04:04.877
and how much value are you storing

00:04:04.877 --> 00:04:05.845
in Bitcoin?

00:04:05.845 --> 00:04:07.413
If somebody is only doing

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:08.381
pocket money,

00:04:08.381 --> 00:04:10.617
maybe $100 a few hundred dollars,

00:04:10.617 --> 00:04:11.484
just download

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:14.487
one of the free apps out there

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:15.054
and then you have

00:04:15.054 --> 00:04:17.190
your private keys on your phone.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.658
You know, that is a hot wallet.

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:19.559
It is.

00:04:19.559 --> 00:04:20.426
It is one of

00:04:20.426 --> 00:04:22.328
the least secure ways of doing it.

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:23.796
But it is

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:24.597
in many ways

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:26.532
still better than having your keys

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:27.567
with a third party.

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:28.968
If you're going up a level,

00:04:28.968 --> 00:04:29.702
if you have, you know,

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:30.837
thousands of dollars,

00:04:30.837 --> 00:04:32.138
a non-trivial amount,

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:33.640
that's when you probably want to

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.707
invest $50

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:35.541
to $100

00:04:35.541 --> 00:04:37.910
in a dedicated hardware device.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:38.678
There's a number of them

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:40.113
out there on the market.

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:41.347
None of them are perfect.

00:04:41.347 --> 00:04:43.483
They all have pros and cons,

00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:44.617
strengths and weaknesses.

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:45.351
But really,

00:04:45.351 --> 00:04:47.086
any of them is better than

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:48.121
none of them.

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:49.289
Better than having,

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:51.224
you know, your keys

00:04:51.224 --> 00:04:52.592
either with a trusted third party

00:04:52.592 --> 00:04:54.060
or just on an

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:55.295
Internet connected device,

00:04:55.295 --> 00:04:56.863
which is easier for hackers

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:57.697
to get into.

00:04:57.697 --> 00:04:58.331
My living room

00:04:58.331 --> 00:04:59.032
is going to be littered

00:04:59.032 --> 00:05:00.566
with air gapped devices soon.

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:01.267
Perfect.

00:05:01.267 --> 00:05:02.201
Yeah, And

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:04.003
especially if you want to

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:05.071
become a power user

00:05:05.071 --> 00:05:07.140
or just try a number of them out.

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:07.707
Like I said,

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:08.941
they all offer

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:09.909
different security models,

00:05:09.909 --> 00:05:11.978
different user experiences.

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:12.312
You know,

00:05:12.312 --> 00:05:13.946
I have drawers and drawers

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:14.781
full of different ones

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:15.882
and I have plenty

00:05:15.882 --> 00:05:17.050
of opinions around

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:18.951
which ones I like and dislike

00:05:18.951 --> 00:05:20.887
or would recommend to people.

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:24.090
But then really the next tier is-

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:25.858
the best way I can describe

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:26.759
it is

00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:28.227
you're trying to eliminate

00:05:28.227 --> 00:05:29.429
every possible

00:05:29.429 --> 00:05:31.230
single point of failure.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:33.966
The problem with these bearer

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:35.101
assets,

00:05:35.101 --> 00:05:38.037
the problem with empowering people

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:39.372
with cryptography,

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:42.375
is that we're essentially handing

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.377
toxic waste to them

00:05:44.377 --> 00:05:46.412
or radioactive material,

00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:47.380
if you will.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.383
It's a private key that unlocks

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:51.517
the functionality

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:52.652
to these assets,

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:54.120
these different networks.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.154
And, you know,

00:05:55.154 --> 00:05:56.089
as long as you can keep

00:05:56.089 --> 00:05:58.691
that tiny amount of data safe

00:05:58.691 --> 00:06:00.493
so that nobody other than yourself

00:06:00.493 --> 00:06:01.994
has access to it,

00:06:01.994 --> 00:06:03.863
you're sovereign, you know,

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:04.964
you're immensely powerful

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:06.032
within that network.

00:06:06.032 --> 00:06:06.466
You're able

00:06:06.466 --> 00:06:07.767
to use the functionality

00:06:07.767 --> 00:06:08.968
of that network.

00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:11.170
But as soon as somebody

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:11.938
gets access

00:06:11.938 --> 00:06:13.339
to that little bit of data,

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:14.507
they can basically

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:15.541
step into your shoes

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:17.877
and generally what happens is

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:19.178
catastrophic loss.

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:20.146
You know,

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:22.982
one second and you have everything.

00:06:22.982 --> 00:06:24.484
The next second, it's all gone

00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:26.219
and there is no authority

00:06:26.219 --> 00:06:27.920
that can get it back for you.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:28.354
So it's,

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:28.554
you know,

00:06:28.554 --> 00:06:29.088
to eliminate

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:30.123
single points of failure,

00:06:30.123 --> 00:06:31.524
we have to implement

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:32.759
best practices.

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:34.394
We have to leverage

00:06:34.394 --> 00:06:36.162
functionality of these protocols

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:37.263
to basically

00:06:37.263 --> 00:06:38.898
use multiple different keys

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:40.833
instead of just having one key.

00:06:40.833 --> 00:06:42.101
And so

00:06:42.101 --> 00:06:42.869
this is generally

00:06:42.869 --> 00:06:44.370
called multi-signature.

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:45.571
You know, it's functionality

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:46.105
that a lot of these

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:47.640
protocols offer.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.409
And that just means that

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:50.877
you create keys

00:06:50.877 --> 00:06:51.511
and you store them

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:52.745
in different places

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:54.614
on different types of hardware.

00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:56.048
And you can

00:06:56.048 --> 00:06:57.450
you can create resiliency

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:58.684
and redundancy.

00:06:58.684 --> 00:06:59.919
And this is really

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:00.553
what I've been doing

00:07:00.553 --> 00:07:01.788
for almost eight years now

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.289
in a couple of different companies,

00:07:03.289 --> 00:07:04.991
couple of different capacities.

00:07:04.991 --> 00:07:06.092
It's trying to make it easier

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:09.095
for non-technical and normal people

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:10.029
to put themselves

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:11.330
into that position

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.299
and basically create

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:13.966
what I consider

00:07:13.966 --> 00:07:15.601
to be a better than bank

00:07:15.601 --> 00:07:16.803
level of security.

00:07:16.803 --> 00:07:17.804
Because if you think about it,

00:07:17.804 --> 00:07:20.807
even the most secure bank vault,

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:23.543
even, you know,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.279
a fortress that is, you know,

00:07:26.279 --> 00:07:29.282
guarded by military,

00:07:30.049 --> 00:07:30.616
that is like

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:31.984
a single physical location,

00:07:31.984 --> 00:07:33.219
which is in itself

00:07:33.219 --> 00:07:35.087
still a single point of failure,

00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:36.556
but with cryptography,

00:07:36.556 --> 00:07:37.590
with private keys.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:39.992
Now we can actually decentralize

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:41.260
your security

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.328
and make it so

00:07:42.328 --> 00:07:43.396
that, you know, an attacker

00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:44.864
would actually have to compromise

00:07:44.864 --> 00:07:46.299
many different points.

00:07:46.299 --> 00:07:47.800
And this just creates

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:48.601
a level of security

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.369
that's never been possible before.

00:07:50.369 --> 00:07:51.471
So you were profiled,

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:52.672
somewhat surprisingly,

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:54.607
by The New York Times,

00:07:54.607 --> 00:07:56.509
specifically about your attempts

00:07:56.509 --> 00:07:58.845
to evade the surveillance state.

00:07:58.845 --> 00:07:59.479
What steps

00:07:59.479 --> 00:08:01.547
did you take to disappear yourself?

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:02.648
It was nearly a year

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:06.352
long process of investigating

00:08:06.352 --> 00:08:08.654
exactly all the different ways

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:10.756
that our data is leaked.

00:08:10.756 --> 00:08:13.392
Now, I had a fair amount of

00:08:13.392 --> 00:08:15.595
of background of understanding

00:08:15.595 --> 00:08:17.263
how your data, how you get tracked

00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:18.464
and how your data gets leaked,

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:19.832
just as you're doing

00:08:19.832 --> 00:08:20.967
normal Internet

00:08:20.967 --> 00:08:22.235
browsing activity

00:08:22.235 --> 00:08:22.602
is because

00:08:22.602 --> 00:08:23.669
I spent ten years

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:25.137
working at an email

00:08:25.137 --> 00:08:26.405
marketing company.

00:08:26.405 --> 00:08:27.740
I was the guy

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:29.509
collecting all of your data

00:08:29.509 --> 00:08:31.477
and running analytics on it.

00:08:31.477 --> 00:08:33.946
And as a result,

00:08:33.946 --> 00:08:35.181
I'm quite aware of just, you know,

00:08:35.181 --> 00:08:37.083
how many trackers there are

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:37.884
whenever you're doing

00:08:37.884 --> 00:08:39.285
anything on the Internet.

00:08:39.285 --> 00:08:40.853
So, you know, there was,

00:08:40.853 --> 00:08:43.456
there was that level of technical

00:08:43.456 --> 00:08:44.824
sophistication of just

00:08:44.824 --> 00:08:46.659
trying to block stuff,

00:08:46.659 --> 00:08:48.160
ad blockers, VPNs,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.929
so on and so forth.

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:52.064
But that's actually the easy part.

00:08:52.064 --> 00:08:55.535
The hard part is more on the legal

00:08:55.535 --> 00:08:56.903
side, the government side.

00:08:58.404 --> 00:08:59.639
The most difficult

00:08:59.639 --> 00:09:01.374
being, of course,

00:09:01.374 --> 00:09:03.576
your driver's license,

00:09:03.576 --> 00:09:04.176
things that are

00:09:04.176 --> 00:09:07.179
tied to your ownership of,

00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:07.880
you know, quote

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:09.649
unquote, real assets. Like a house.

00:09:09.649 --> 00:09:11.317
Like a house, like a car,

00:09:11.317 --> 00:09:11.484
you know,

00:09:11.484 --> 00:09:13.052
anything that you have to register

00:09:13.052 --> 00:09:14.253
with the state.

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:16.155
That's where it gets really tricky

00:09:16.155 --> 00:09:17.390
and it becomes

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:19.392
jurisdiction specific.

00:09:19.392 --> 00:09:19.926
But, you know,

00:09:19.926 --> 00:09:21.961
there are states where it is

00:09:21.961 --> 00:09:23.763
possible to create trusts.

00:09:23.763 --> 00:09:24.597
It's possible

00:09:24.597 --> 00:09:26.566
to create corporations

00:09:26.566 --> 00:09:27.633
that you can use

00:09:27.633 --> 00:09:30.202
as proxies or shields

00:09:30.202 --> 00:09:31.904
to essentially hide

00:09:31.904 --> 00:09:33.806
the true name, the true ownership.

00:09:33.806 --> 00:09:35.441
Unfortunately, this is not simple.

00:09:35.441 --> 00:09:36.475
It's not cheap.

00:09:36.475 --> 00:09:37.076
It requires

00:09:37.076 --> 00:09:40.079
time and money and lawyers.

00:09:40.179 --> 00:09:42.481
But if you do it

00:09:42.481 --> 00:09:45.184
and you’re

00:09:45.184 --> 00:09:48.054
extremely diligent about it,

00:09:48.054 --> 00:09:48.988
you can,

00:09:48.988 --> 00:09:50.089
you know, button up

00:09:50.089 --> 00:09:51.090
all of the holes,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.491
as it were, and prevent

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:54.126
any of the the data from leaking.

00:09:54.126 --> 00:09:55.962
But it requires an ongoing effort.

00:09:55.962 --> 00:09:57.296
Did you make an LLC?

00:09:57.296 --> 00:09:58.197
Oh, yes, I have.

00:09:58.197 --> 00:09:58.331
You know,

00:09:58.331 --> 00:09:59.532
a number of different trusts.

00:09:59.532 --> 00:10:00.132
An LLC

00:10:00.132 --> 00:10:04.670
is an- LLCs owned by other LLCs

00:10:04.704 --> 00:10:05.538
that are owned by trusts.

00:10:05.538 --> 00:10:05.705
You know,

00:10:05.705 --> 00:10:06.906
it really is the shell game.

00:10:06.906 --> 00:10:07.440
So you have layers

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:08.441
to this essentially.

00:10:08.441 --> 00:10:08.841
Absolutely.

00:10:08.841 --> 00:10:09.308
Do you have a sense

00:10:09.308 --> 00:10:10.810
of how much it costs

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.378
to do that type of thing?

00:10:12.378 --> 00:10:12.945
Because presumably

00:10:12.945 --> 00:10:13.913
that's a barrier

00:10:13.913 --> 00:10:15.481
that disallows some people

00:10:15.481 --> 00:10:17.083
from actually evading surveillance

00:10:17.083 --> 00:10:18.150
the way they want to.

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:19.251
Generally,

00:10:19.251 --> 00:10:21.454
at least a few thousand dollars.

00:10:21.454 --> 00:10:22.488
Okay.

00:10:22.488 --> 00:10:23.623
But outside of

00:10:23.623 --> 00:10:25.591
just the legal costs,

00:10:25.591 --> 00:10:26.892
I would say the higher ongoing

00:10:26.892 --> 00:10:28.694
maintenance costs

00:10:28.694 --> 00:10:31.397
is with my driver's license and my,

00:10:31.397 --> 00:10:33.432
you know, quote unquote, residence.

00:10:33.432 --> 00:10:34.400
The address

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.101
that, you know, the state

00:10:35.101 --> 00:10:36.335
considers to be my residence.

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:37.603
And unfortunately,

00:10:37.603 --> 00:10:38.337
the only way

00:10:38.337 --> 00:10:39.205
that I've really been able

00:10:39.205 --> 00:10:40.072
to find a solution to

00:10:40.072 --> 00:10:43.275
that is literally find the cheapest

00:10:43.909 --> 00:10:46.479
apartment, house, hovel or what

00:10:46.479 --> 00:10:48.114
have you that has, you know,

00:10:48.114 --> 00:10:50.316
a physical mailbox.

00:10:50.316 --> 00:10:51.717
There are ways that you can get

00:10:51.717 --> 00:10:53.653
really creative.

00:10:53.653 --> 00:10:55.087
The shortest version of

00:10:55.087 --> 00:10:56.956
I guess a way to describe it is

00:10:56.956 --> 00:10:58.190
you look into

00:10:59.425 --> 00:11:00.860
RV life

00:11:00.860 --> 00:11:03.863
forums or digital nomad forums.

00:11:03.896 --> 00:11:06.999
There are some states out there,

00:11:07.099 --> 00:11:09.635
I think Nebraska, perhaps,

00:11:09.635 --> 00:11:11.370
where you can very easily,

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:12.872
in a matter of a few weeks,

00:11:12.872 --> 00:11:14.240
get a driver's license

00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:15.441
and establish quote

00:11:15.441 --> 00:11:16.742
unquote residency.

00:11:16.742 --> 00:11:17.543
But it just depends on

00:11:17.543 --> 00:11:18.377
how much effort

00:11:18.377 --> 00:11:19.812
you're willing to put into it.

00:11:19.812 --> 00:11:21.047
Both self-custody

00:11:21.047 --> 00:11:22.848
and disappearing yourself

00:11:22.848 --> 00:11:23.749
require, like

00:11:23.749 --> 00:11:25.818
from the panopticon, essentially,

00:11:25.818 --> 00:11:27.486
require a pretty high

00:11:27.486 --> 00:11:28.721
level of technical skill,

00:11:28.721 --> 00:11:30.589
obviously disappearing yourself

00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:31.123
from the state

00:11:31.123 --> 00:11:32.291
the way that you did requires

00:11:32.291 --> 00:11:34.660
so much more skill than

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:35.761
you know, self-custody.

00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:37.029
Is there any way that this can

00:11:37.029 --> 00:11:39.465
actually catch on en masse?

00:11:39.465 --> 00:11:42.068
Yeah, I am definitely bearish

00:11:42.068 --> 00:11:43.703
on this

00:11:43.703 --> 00:11:44.370
being adopted

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:45.705
even by more than a small

00:11:45.705 --> 00:11:47.606
contingent of people.

00:11:47.606 --> 00:11:47.907
I would say

00:11:47.907 --> 00:11:48.941
it's really relegated

00:11:48.941 --> 00:11:50.943
more to like the celebrities

00:11:50.943 --> 00:11:52.044
and the ultra wealthy,

00:11:52.044 --> 00:11:54.346
you know, the people who

00:11:54.346 --> 00:11:55.414
for one reason or other,

00:11:55.414 --> 00:11:56.682
they may have stalkers,

00:11:56.682 --> 00:11:57.750
they may suspect that

00:11:57.750 --> 00:11:59.251
they will have stalkers

00:11:59.251 --> 00:12:01.921
or they're just high profile enough

00:12:01.921 --> 00:12:03.622
and have enough wealth

00:12:03.622 --> 00:12:04.056
that, you know,

00:12:04.056 --> 00:12:06.392
spending thousands of dollars

00:12:06.392 --> 00:12:07.626
doesn't really mean much to them.

00:12:07.626 --> 00:12:08.761
You know, it is

00:12:08.761 --> 00:12:11.397
it is a form of insurance policy.

00:12:11.397 --> 00:12:12.164
And, you know,

00:12:12.164 --> 00:12:13.466
when it comes to both privacy

00:12:13.466 --> 00:12:14.633
and security,

00:12:14.633 --> 00:12:17.203
unfortunately, my experience over

00:12:17.203 --> 00:12:19.071
the past decade has been that

00:12:19.071 --> 00:12:20.973
basically nobody values

00:12:20.973 --> 00:12:22.174
their privacy and security

00:12:22.174 --> 00:12:24.176
until it's too late. Self-custody.

00:12:24.176 --> 00:12:26.011
Is that something that is likely

00:12:26.011 --> 00:12:26.979
to catch on

00:12:26.979 --> 00:12:27.713
with the general public,

00:12:27.713 --> 00:12:29.048
or do you think the barrier might

00:12:29.048 --> 00:12:30.916
still be too high?

00:12:30.916 --> 00:12:33.219
There is a high barrier.

00:12:33.219 --> 00:12:33.652
Like I said,

00:12:33.652 --> 00:12:34.854
I've been trying to bring it down

00:12:34.854 --> 00:12:37.056
for eight years and it's-

00:12:37.056 --> 00:12:38.557
there's no end in sight to that.

00:12:38.557 --> 00:12:41.560
But really part of the problem is

00:12:41.994 --> 00:12:44.096
the level of advantage

00:12:44.096 --> 00:12:45.965
that custodians and trusted

00:12:45.965 --> 00:12:47.333
third parties have

00:12:47.333 --> 00:12:49.335
when it comes to convenience

00:12:49.335 --> 00:12:50.369
and user experience.

00:12:50.369 --> 00:12:52.271
It's because they control

00:12:52.271 --> 00:12:53.939
the entire experience.

00:12:53.939 --> 00:12:55.341
Do you think there's ever

00:12:56.342 --> 00:12:57.576
a future where we can get

00:12:57.576 --> 00:12:59.345
grandmas to self-custody?

00:12:59.345 --> 00:13:00.613
Well, it's definitely possible.

00:13:00.613 --> 00:13:00.846
And, you know,

00:13:00.846 --> 00:13:02.515
I can tell you from experience,

00:13:02.515 --> 00:13:04.250
we have plenty of people

00:13:04.250 --> 00:13:05.551
in their eighties

00:13:05.551 --> 00:13:07.920
who are clients of Casa.

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:08.954
We have

00:13:08.954 --> 00:13:09.388
you know,

00:13:09.388 --> 00:13:10.356
we have combined

00:13:10.356 --> 00:13:10.523
you know,

00:13:10.523 --> 00:13:12.491
both the user experience

00:13:12.491 --> 00:13:14.960
and a level of service.

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:16.629
So it's possible,

00:13:16.629 --> 00:13:18.297
but there's

00:13:18.297 --> 00:13:20.499
just the question of incentives.

00:13:20.499 --> 00:13:21.567
And so,

00:13:21.567 --> 00:13:22.668
you know, part of the problem

00:13:22.668 --> 00:13:23.702
also is just

00:13:23.702 --> 00:13:25.938
what is the onboarding process

00:13:25.938 --> 00:13:26.772
look like?

00:13:26.772 --> 00:13:27.406
If someone wants to

00:13:27.406 --> 00:13:29.108
get into Bitcoin right

00:13:29.108 --> 00:13:30.242
now, probably

00:13:30.242 --> 00:13:31.811
99% of people

00:13:31.811 --> 00:13:32.745
get onboarded

00:13:32.745 --> 00:13:34.880
by signing up at an exchange.

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:35.447
Yeah.

00:13:35.447 --> 00:13:37.016
They are, you know,

00:13:37.016 --> 00:13:37.616
start off

00:13:37.616 --> 00:13:39.151
using a trusted third party

00:13:39.151 --> 00:13:40.953
and usually that's also

00:13:40.953 --> 00:13:42.221
where they stop.

00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:44.456
They either don't look into

00:13:44.456 --> 00:13:45.424
or they don't

00:13:45.424 --> 00:13:47.193
understand that, you know,

00:13:47.193 --> 00:13:48.561
withdrawing to

00:13:48.561 --> 00:13:50.362
self-custody is an option.

00:13:50.362 --> 00:13:51.864
Or if they do see it as an option,

00:13:51.864 --> 00:13:52.665
it's just too scary.

00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:54.066
They don't want to deal with it.

00:13:54.066 --> 00:13:57.036
So I am, I would say, more bullish

00:13:57.036 --> 00:13:58.904
on a future

00:13:58.904 --> 00:14:01.874
in which we have more on ramps,

00:14:01.874 --> 00:14:02.341
by which

00:14:02.341 --> 00:14:03.108
I mean

00:14:03.108 --> 00:14:04.977
people are engaging in commerce

00:14:04.977 --> 00:14:05.945
and they're just directly

00:14:05.945 --> 00:14:07.112
receiving Bitcoin

00:14:07.112 --> 00:14:08.080
and probably over

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:09.281
Lightning Network.

00:14:09.281 --> 00:14:09.782
And, you know,

00:14:09.782 --> 00:14:10.516
that's

00:14:10.516 --> 00:14:11.450
something that we're starting

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:12.451
to see happen,

00:14:12.451 --> 00:14:13.953
especially as we're seeing,

00:14:13.953 --> 00:14:15.888
for example, Nostr,

00:14:15.888 --> 00:14:18.023
a new protocol, a new network

00:14:18.023 --> 00:14:19.325
that has come online

00:14:19.325 --> 00:14:20.125
that is, is

00:14:20.125 --> 00:14:21.227
really more of a Lightning

00:14:21.227 --> 00:14:22.561
native experience.

00:14:22.561 --> 00:14:25.164
And with that being integrated

00:14:25.164 --> 00:14:27.032
into a lot of the clients,

00:14:27.032 --> 00:14:28.000
people are very easily

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:29.101
just setting up, you know,

00:14:29.101 --> 00:14:30.502
self-custody wallets

00:14:30.502 --> 00:14:32.605
and directly receiving money

00:14:32.605 --> 00:14:33.906
without ever signing up

00:14:33.906 --> 00:14:36.008
on a custodian or an exchange.

00:14:36.008 --> 00:14:36.842
So Nostr’s

00:14:36.842 --> 00:14:37.810
sort of the gateway drug.

00:14:37.810 --> 00:14:38.711
Yeah, it's

00:14:38.711 --> 00:14:40.412
one of many possible futures,

00:14:40.412 --> 00:14:40.913
and I think there's

00:14:40.913 --> 00:14:42.681
many different ways that we can,

00:14:42.681 --> 00:14:43.082
you know,

00:14:43.082 --> 00:14:44.650
create new experiences

00:14:44.650 --> 00:14:46.352
where people are getting onboarded

00:14:46.352 --> 00:14:46.919
directly

00:14:46.919 --> 00:14:48.721
into a self-custody environment.

00:14:48.721 --> 00:14:50.389
But I think we will have a diverse

00:14:50.389 --> 00:14:53.392
ecosystem and range of custody,

00:14:53.525 --> 00:14:55.394
and it doesn't actually

00:14:55.394 --> 00:14:57.096
necessarily need to be a

00:14:57.096 --> 00:14:57.963
binary thing.

00:14:57.963 --> 00:15:00.032
It doesn't have to be just trusted

00:15:00.032 --> 00:15:02.868
third party or just self-custody.

00:15:02.868 --> 00:15:03.736
And this is actually where

00:15:03.736 --> 00:15:06.372
some of the recent innovations in

00:15:06.372 --> 00:15:07.473
Bitcoin scripting

00:15:07.473 --> 00:15:08.641
and programing language

00:15:08.641 --> 00:15:10.509
are becoming very interesting

00:15:10.509 --> 00:15:11.377
to me

00:15:11.377 --> 00:15:12.878
as a builder of

00:15:12.878 --> 00:15:14.747
self-custody products.

00:15:14.747 --> 00:15:16.348
But suffice to say

00:15:16.348 --> 00:15:17.950
some of the other ways

00:15:17.950 --> 00:15:19.051
that you could

00:15:20.119 --> 00:15:21.420
try to find

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:22.621
a happy medium

00:15:22.621 --> 00:15:24.423
between these two extremes

00:15:24.423 --> 00:15:25.991
are things like

00:15:25.991 --> 00:15:27.660
hybrid custody,

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:28.594
where you have

00:15:28.594 --> 00:15:30.362
multiple keys that are shared

00:15:30.362 --> 00:15:32.831
perhaps between you, family,

00:15:32.831 --> 00:15:35.834
other semi trusted experts.

00:15:36.969 --> 00:15:38.037
I think that, you know,

00:15:38.037 --> 00:15:40.272
there is a push happening

00:15:40.272 --> 00:15:42.708
towards community banking models.

00:15:42.708 --> 00:15:43.342
This is something

00:15:43.342 --> 00:15:44.576
that we're seeing,

00:15:44.576 --> 00:15:45.177
for example,

00:15:45.177 --> 00:15:47.313
with the Fedimint project,

00:15:47.313 --> 00:15:49.615
you know, Fedi is building software

00:15:49.615 --> 00:15:51.684
that's making it easier for,

00:15:51.684 --> 00:15:52.017
you know,

00:15:52.017 --> 00:15:53.519
your local family

00:15:53.519 --> 00:15:55.821
or your local community bank

00:15:55.821 --> 00:15:57.156
to be providing

00:15:57.156 --> 00:15:57.957
what you know,

00:15:57.957 --> 00:15:59.792
that would still be a third party

00:15:59.792 --> 00:16:02.761
custody model, but that would

00:16:02.861 --> 00:16:05.731
hopefully create a world in which

00:16:05.731 --> 00:16:07.533
instead of having ten

00:16:07.533 --> 00:16:09.034
or 100 custodians

00:16:09.034 --> 00:16:11.570
holding the majority of Bitcoin

00:16:11.570 --> 00:16:12.571
for everyone,

00:16:12.571 --> 00:16:14.540
we would have thousands,

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:15.274
if not tens

00:16:15.274 --> 00:16:18.277
or hundreds of thousands of smaller

00:16:18.344 --> 00:16:20.145
regional custodians

00:16:20.145 --> 00:16:22.414
that are helping people

00:16:22.414 --> 00:16:23.415
manage that money.

00:16:23.415 --> 00:16:25.784
So the point being, you know,

00:16:25.784 --> 00:16:28.320
it still gives

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:30.756
some risk to the end users.

00:16:30.756 --> 00:16:32.524
But from a really high level,

00:16:32.524 --> 00:16:33.726
you don't have the same level

00:16:33.726 --> 00:16:35.461
of systemic risk.

00:16:35.461 --> 00:16:35.928
And that's

00:16:35.928 --> 00:16:37.663
what really worries me

00:16:37.663 --> 00:16:40.632
is if too much of the Bitcoin

00:16:40.632 --> 00:16:42.134
goes into too few hands,

00:16:42.134 --> 00:16:43.102
especially if they're like

00:16:43.102 --> 00:16:45.270
regulated companies

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:45.971
that can be easily

00:16:45.971 --> 00:16:47.706
targeted by nation states,

00:16:47.706 --> 00:16:50.709
that is when Bitcoin itself,

00:16:50.843 --> 00:16:54.079
you know, as a network becomes

00:16:54.747 --> 00:16:56.749
a bit more prone to, you know,

00:16:56.749 --> 00:16:58.217
nation state actions.

00:16:58.217 --> 00:16:59.151
Do you self-custody

00:16:59.151 --> 00:17:00.386
all of your crypto?

00:17:00.386 --> 00:17:00.986
Not all.

00:17:00.986 --> 00:17:01.153
You know,

00:17:01.153 --> 00:17:02.221
I have a

00:17:02.221 --> 00:17:05.557
diverse range of different set ups.

00:17:05.657 --> 00:17:06.859
What does that breakdown look like?

00:17:06.859 --> 00:17:07.960
Like how do you make that decision

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.862
about how much to self-custody?

00:17:09.862 --> 00:17:10.429
Well, you know,

00:17:10.429 --> 00:17:13.165
some of it is actually legal.

00:17:13.165 --> 00:17:15.601
For example, you know, retirement

00:17:15.601 --> 00:17:16.201
funds,

00:17:16.201 --> 00:17:19.171
it can be much more difficult to

00:17:19.204 --> 00:17:21.273
to have, you know, self-custody

00:17:21.273 --> 00:17:21.940
Bitcoin

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:24.309
as a part of a retirement fund.

00:17:24.309 --> 00:17:26.578
It's certainly far more

00:17:27.946 --> 00:17:28.981
easy to

00:17:28.981 --> 00:17:29.581
use, you know,

00:17:29.581 --> 00:17:30.416
one of the products

00:17:30.416 --> 00:17:32.217
that's out there on the market.

00:17:32.217 --> 00:17:33.585
What kinds of changes

00:17:33.585 --> 00:17:34.887
to Bitcoin's

00:17:34.887 --> 00:17:36.121
protocol are needed

00:17:36.121 --> 00:17:37.656
in the coming years?

00:17:37.656 --> 00:17:40.025
It was very controversial topic

00:17:40.025 --> 00:17:41.660
to say that Bitcoin needs

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:43.162
anything at all.

00:17:43.162 --> 00:17:44.797
Yeah, but, you know.

00:17:44.797 --> 00:17:47.800
Do you reject the premise?

00:17:48.400 --> 00:17:50.502
So I would say, you

00:17:50.502 --> 00:17:51.136
know, Bitcoin

00:17:51.136 --> 00:17:53.972
doesn't need anything to survive.

00:17:53.972 --> 00:17:54.306
Mm hmm.

00:17:54.306 --> 00:17:56.041
But as a technologist,

00:17:56.041 --> 00:17:57.443
I believe that in order

00:17:57.443 --> 00:17:59.044
for Bitcoin to thrive

00:17:59.044 --> 00:18:01.313
and to become a

00:18:01.313 --> 00:18:02.314
more developer

00:18:02.314 --> 00:18:05.684
friendly, more secure, or at least

00:18:05.684 --> 00:18:06.718
secure in terms

00:18:06.718 --> 00:18:09.121
of like end user security

00:18:09.121 --> 00:18:09.855
security models

00:18:09.855 --> 00:18:13.025
for self-custody, more scalable,

00:18:13.625 --> 00:18:16.762
then we do want to see some changes

00:18:16.762 --> 00:18:17.996
that will make it possible

00:18:17.996 --> 00:18:20.332
for more permissionless innovation.

00:18:20.332 --> 00:18:21.433
Basically,

00:18:21.433 --> 00:18:21.633
you know,

00:18:21.633 --> 00:18:22.935
we don't want people

00:18:22.935 --> 00:18:24.236
to have to screw around

00:18:24.236 --> 00:18:25.471
with the Bitcoin protocol

00:18:25.471 --> 00:18:27.306
and make a lot of changes to it,

00:18:27.306 --> 00:18:28.574
that's very contentious.

00:18:28.574 --> 00:18:31.610
We went through multiyear debates

00:18:31.610 --> 00:18:33.312
around making some changes

00:18:33.312 --> 00:18:35.013
to Bitcoin a few years ago.

00:18:35.013 --> 00:18:36.782
But what we're lacking

00:18:36.782 --> 00:18:38.250
is the ability for people

00:18:38.250 --> 00:18:38.817
to go out

00:18:38.817 --> 00:18:40.185
and easily create

00:18:40.185 --> 00:18:42.621
other layers of technologies

00:18:42.621 --> 00:18:43.922
that are cryptographically

00:18:43.922 --> 00:18:45.190
linked to Bitcoin

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:46.391
where they can basically use

00:18:46.391 --> 00:18:47.326
those as playgrounds

00:18:47.326 --> 00:18:48.660
for experimentation

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:49.528
and be able to do

00:18:49.528 --> 00:18:50.195
whatever they want

00:18:50.195 --> 00:18:52.331
without having to ask permission

00:18:52.331 --> 00:18:53.732
to change Bitcoin,

00:18:53.732 --> 00:18:55.234
without having to worry

00:18:55.234 --> 00:18:56.401
about accidentally

00:18:56.401 --> 00:18:58.003
doing something that breaks

00:18:58.003 --> 00:18:59.071
Bitcoin itself.

00:18:59.071 --> 00:18:59.671
Well, so explain

00:18:59.671 --> 00:19:00.606
a little bit of the controversy

00:19:00.606 --> 00:19:01.373
surrounding this.

00:19:01.373 --> 00:19:05.344
Without making

00:19:05.344 --> 00:19:06.645
any new enemies, ideally?

00:19:06.645 --> 00:19:07.045
Yeah.

00:19:07.045 --> 00:19:08.147
Well, you know,

00:19:08.147 --> 00:19:11.016
one example is that,

00:19:11.016 --> 00:19:11.250
you know,

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:13.051
whenever you change software,

00:19:13.051 --> 00:19:14.987
especially when you're changing

00:19:14.987 --> 00:19:15.921
something like Bitcoin,

00:19:15.921 --> 00:19:16.388
which is it

00:19:16.388 --> 00:19:17.823
is programable money, you know,

00:19:17.823 --> 00:19:19.825
it does have a programing language,

00:19:19.825 --> 00:19:21.393
even though it's very basic

00:19:21.393 --> 00:19:22.761
in what it can do.

00:19:22.761 --> 00:19:22.995
You know,

00:19:22.995 --> 00:19:24.062
whenever you're creating

00:19:24.062 --> 00:19:25.931
new functionality

00:19:25.931 --> 00:19:27.065
that is going to be leveraged

00:19:27.065 --> 00:19:28.100
by developers,

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:29.668
it's always possible

00:19:29.668 --> 00:19:30.702
that that functionality

00:19:30.702 --> 00:19:33.071
could be used in unintended ways.

00:19:33.071 --> 00:19:33.872
And so we've had a little

00:19:33.872 --> 00:19:35.040
bit of controversy

00:19:35.040 --> 00:19:36.975
just over the past month or two

00:19:36.975 --> 00:19:37.809
with some people

00:19:37.809 --> 00:19:38.977
leveraging functionality

00:19:38.977 --> 00:19:41.046
that got activated a few years ago

00:19:41.046 --> 00:19:42.347
to do new things

00:19:42.347 --> 00:19:44.349
that had not been anticipated.

00:19:44.349 --> 00:19:46.485
And you could argue that

00:19:46.485 --> 00:19:48.453
it was not the

00:19:48.453 --> 00:19:50.556
intended purpose of the function.

00:19:50.556 --> 00:19:51.690
Like what?

00:19:51.690 --> 00:19:53.025
So basically,

00:19:53.025 --> 00:19:53.192
you know,

00:19:53.192 --> 00:19:54.960
people have discovered

00:19:54.960 --> 00:19:56.628
new ways of storing data

00:19:56.628 --> 00:19:58.030
in the Bitcoin blockchain

00:19:58.030 --> 00:20:01.033
that it wasn't as easy to do

00:20:01.033 --> 00:20:01.567
before.

00:20:01.567 --> 00:20:02.768
It was possible,

00:20:02.768 --> 00:20:04.369
but it just wasn't as easy.

00:20:04.369 --> 00:20:04.937
And, you know,

00:20:04.937 --> 00:20:06.271
some people get upset

00:20:06.271 --> 00:20:08.073
when Bitcoin is used for,

00:20:08.073 --> 00:20:10.709
you know, non-monetary

00:20:10.709 --> 00:20:12.110
transactions and purposes

00:20:12.110 --> 00:20:13.212
and data storage.

00:20:13.212 --> 00:20:14.713
But I'm a technologist,

00:20:14.713 --> 00:20:16.615
so I've always looked at Bitcoin

00:20:16.615 --> 00:20:18.717
as a type of database.

00:20:18.717 --> 00:20:20.852
It's just the

00:20:20.852 --> 00:20:22.287
the least efficient

00:20:22.287 --> 00:20:23.255
type of database

00:20:23.255 --> 00:20:23.889
that I've ever seen.

00:20:23.889 --> 00:20:25.457
So the most difficult database

00:20:25.457 --> 00:20:26.992
to try to work with.

00:20:26.992 --> 00:20:29.995
But as a database, as a

00:20:30.362 --> 00:20:32.965
a store of historical records,

00:20:32.965 --> 00:20:33.532
it provides

00:20:33.532 --> 00:20:36.435
some very interesting guarantees

00:20:36.435 --> 00:20:37.402
that no database

00:20:37.402 --> 00:20:39.004
has ever been able to do before.

00:20:39.004 --> 00:20:39.972
I assume you're

00:20:39.972 --> 00:20:40.739
talking a little bit

00:20:40.739 --> 00:20:42.708
about ordinals, the ability

00:20:42.708 --> 00:20:45.277
to put JPEGs on the blockchain

00:20:45.277 --> 00:20:46.712
or what have you.

00:20:46.712 --> 00:20:48.513
What do you think of that? Right.

00:20:48.513 --> 00:20:50.349
I mean, I'm quite neutral.

00:20:50.349 --> 00:20:51.817
I'm ambivalent on it.

00:20:51.817 --> 00:20:53.252
I'm personally not

00:20:53.252 --> 00:20:57.456
interested in NFTs or

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:00.726
trading other tokens that

00:21:00.726 --> 00:21:02.394
people are creating

00:21:02.394 --> 00:21:04.429
using the Bitcoin blockchain

00:21:04.429 --> 00:21:05.464
to do that.

00:21:05.464 --> 00:21:08.467
But I'm a free market proponent,

00:21:08.700 --> 00:21:11.069
so I'm not against people

00:21:11.069 --> 00:21:12.771
developing new functionality

00:21:12.771 --> 00:21:13.605
and new ways

00:21:13.605 --> 00:21:16.475
of having economic interactions

00:21:16.475 --> 00:21:17.843
using Bitcoin

00:21:17.843 --> 00:21:19.745
and related technology.

00:21:19.745 --> 00:21:22.981
This is, this is controversial

00:21:22.981 --> 00:21:25.150
because I think there's

00:21:25.150 --> 00:21:26.785
generally two perspectives

00:21:26.785 --> 00:21:27.819
that are being taken

00:21:27.819 --> 00:21:29.988
with regard to how bitcoin

00:21:29.988 --> 00:21:31.890
quote unquote should be used.

00:21:31.890 --> 00:21:32.524
It's, you know,

00:21:32.524 --> 00:21:35.761
how do you try to describe

00:21:35.761 --> 00:21:37.095
or define,

00:21:37.095 --> 00:21:39.031
quote unquote spam,

00:21:39.031 --> 00:21:40.132
you know, spammy

00:21:40.132 --> 00:21:41.300
use of the blockchain?

00:21:41.300 --> 00:21:43.001
Because the Bitcoin blockchain

00:21:43.001 --> 00:21:45.937
is a scarce shared resource.

00:21:45.937 --> 00:21:47.939
And you can certainly run

00:21:47.939 --> 00:21:49.308
into a sort of tragedy

00:21:49.308 --> 00:21:50.609
of the commons situation

00:21:50.609 --> 00:21:52.611
whenever you have a scarce

00:21:52.611 --> 00:21:54.046
shared resource.

00:21:54.046 --> 00:21:55.947
So on one hand,

00:21:55.947 --> 00:21:56.715
you have people

00:21:56.715 --> 00:21:58.350
who essentially are

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:02.521
placing subjective arguments around

00:22:02.521 --> 00:22:03.822
what they believe

00:22:03.822 --> 00:22:06.525
should be a Bitcoin transaction,

00:22:06.525 --> 00:22:07.459
you know,

00:22:07.459 --> 00:22:08.727
and generally

00:22:08.727 --> 00:22:10.262
that comes down to, well, a Bitcoin

00:22:10.262 --> 00:22:12.030
transaction should just be sending,

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:12.564
you know,

00:22:12.564 --> 00:22:14.232
Satoshis value

00:22:14.232 --> 00:22:15.901
from one person to another

00:22:15.901 --> 00:22:16.802
and that's it.

00:22:16.802 --> 00:22:18.203
It's not about, you know,

00:22:18.203 --> 00:22:19.137
putting a bunch of data

00:22:19.137 --> 00:22:20.005
into the blockchain

00:22:20.005 --> 00:22:21.506
and then calling it something else

00:22:21.506 --> 00:22:22.407
and then saying it's

00:22:22.407 --> 00:22:23.442
a token transfer.

00:22:23.442 --> 00:22:24.443
They, their,

00:22:24.443 --> 00:22:25.744
you know, subjective perspective

00:22:25.744 --> 00:22:26.378
says that

00:22:26.378 --> 00:22:28.380
that is not the original

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:29.581
intended design

00:22:29.581 --> 00:22:31.083
and use of Bitcoin,

00:22:31.083 --> 00:22:32.951
but as it is with any subjective

00:22:32.951 --> 00:22:33.985
arguments, people will take

00:22:33.985 --> 00:22:34.786
the opposite side

00:22:34.786 --> 00:22:35.520
of that and say, Hey,

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:36.555
you should be able to

00:22:36.555 --> 00:22:37.489
do whatever you want

00:22:37.489 --> 00:22:39.391
as long as the protocol says

00:22:39.391 --> 00:22:40.359
it's valid.

00:22:40.359 --> 00:22:43.261
So I consider the opposite

00:22:43.261 --> 00:22:44.363
side of that usually

00:22:44.363 --> 00:22:45.964
to be more of an objective

00:22:45.964 --> 00:22:47.532
economic argument,

00:22:47.532 --> 00:22:50.836
which is the only,

00:22:50.936 --> 00:22:53.939
the only definition of spam

00:22:54.139 --> 00:22:55.207
when it comes to

00:22:55.207 --> 00:22:56.408
Bitcoin transactions

00:22:56.408 --> 00:22:58.543
and use of the network

00:22:58.543 --> 00:23:01.213
that I can figure is,

00:23:01.213 --> 00:23:03.849
are you paying a competitive fee

00:23:03.849 --> 00:23:05.851
to get into the blockchain?

00:23:05.851 --> 00:23:06.418
You know,

00:23:06.418 --> 00:23:09.354
what are you willing to pay?

00:23:09.354 --> 00:23:10.522
Because

00:23:10.522 --> 00:23:11.690
if you're willing to pay

00:23:11.690 --> 00:23:13.291
more than someone else,

00:23:13.291 --> 00:23:15.227
then that probably means

00:23:15.227 --> 00:23:16.395
that what you're doing,

00:23:16.395 --> 00:23:18.397
you know, is more valuable to you

00:23:18.397 --> 00:23:19.931
than what someone else is doing.

00:23:19.931 --> 00:23:20.699
If they're not willing

00:23:20.699 --> 00:23:21.867
to pay as much.

00:23:21.867 --> 00:23:23.068
And that's

00:23:23.068 --> 00:23:25.404
seems to be the most reasonable way

00:23:25.404 --> 00:23:26.872
to decide, you know,

00:23:26.872 --> 00:23:28.540
what data goes into the blockchain,

00:23:28.540 --> 00:23:29.841
because we cannot

00:23:29.841 --> 00:23:30.842
put the entire world's

00:23:30.842 --> 00:23:32.244
data into the blockchain.

00:23:32.244 --> 00:23:32.978
I'm really curious,

00:23:32.978 --> 00:23:33.678
does Bitcoin

00:23:33.678 --> 00:23:34.980
have a developer shortage,

00:23:34.980 --> 00:23:35.881
especially compared

00:23:35.881 --> 00:23:37.048
to like Ethereum?

00:23:37.048 --> 00:23:38.817
There is definitely

00:23:38.817 --> 00:23:40.952
far fewer developers

00:23:40.952 --> 00:23:42.721
and I think a big part of that

00:23:42.721 --> 00:23:45.157
is because of how much more limited

00:23:45.157 --> 00:23:46.057
the functionality

00:23:46.057 --> 00:23:48.660
is, of how much less

00:23:48.660 --> 00:23:49.861
developer friendly.

00:23:49.861 --> 00:23:51.930
A lot of the tooling is.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:53.432
But that is changing.

00:23:53.432 --> 00:23:54.599
We are seeing,

00:23:54.599 --> 00:23:55.834
you know, for example,

00:23:55.834 --> 00:23:56.468
there's something called

00:23:56.468 --> 00:23:57.302
mini script

00:23:57.302 --> 00:23:58.503
that has been developed

00:23:58.503 --> 00:23:59.905
over the past few years

00:23:59.905 --> 00:24:01.173
and I'm seeing more

00:24:01.173 --> 00:24:02.374
and more tooling

00:24:02.374 --> 00:24:04.843
that is coming up around

00:24:04.843 --> 00:24:05.577
making it easier

00:24:05.577 --> 00:24:06.411
for developers

00:24:06.411 --> 00:24:09.347
to create more complex

00:24:09.347 --> 00:24:10.515
locking scripts

00:24:10.515 --> 00:24:11.516
with many different,

00:24:11.516 --> 00:24:11.683
you know,

00:24:11.683 --> 00:24:13.552
logical branches and conditions

00:24:13.552 --> 00:24:15.086
and basically

00:24:15.086 --> 00:24:15.654
allowing us

00:24:15.654 --> 00:24:17.556
to create much more interesting

00:24:17.556 --> 00:24:19.724
self-custody security models.

00:24:19.724 --> 00:24:19.991
Okay,

00:24:19.991 --> 00:24:22.994
so say I were super concerned

00:24:22.994 --> 00:24:24.229
about hyperinflation,

00:24:24.229 --> 00:24:24.763
not to the point

00:24:24.763 --> 00:24:25.564
where I would make any sort of

00:24:25.564 --> 00:24:26.865
like thing like that,

00:24:26.865 --> 00:24:27.933
but how should someone

00:24:27.933 --> 00:24:29.701
prepare for hyper inflation?

00:24:29.701 --> 00:24:30.469
Well,

00:24:30.469 --> 00:24:33.271
you know, as Satoshi said, it, uh,

00:24:33.271 --> 00:24:34.539
it might make sense

00:24:34.539 --> 00:24:35.474
to get a little bit

00:24:35.474 --> 00:24:38.076
just in case this catches on.

00:24:38.076 --> 00:24:39.845
So, you know, holding

00:24:39.845 --> 00:24:41.680
I've always looked at Bitcoin

00:24:41.680 --> 00:24:43.615
from inception

00:24:43.615 --> 00:24:44.516
of when I got into it.

00:24:44.516 --> 00:24:45.550
I've looked at it

00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:47.552
as a sort of hedge,

00:24:47.552 --> 00:24:48.753
a very long term

00:24:48.753 --> 00:24:50.088
hedge, just

00:24:50.088 --> 00:24:50.956
Bitcoin

00:24:50.956 --> 00:24:53.959
does not have any guarantees

00:24:53.959 --> 00:24:57.896
or even have any goals

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.332
with regard to its price

00:25:00.332 --> 00:25:02.100
and its exchange rate.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:04.669
It has no concept of its own

00:25:04.669 --> 00:25:06.171
exchange rate.

00:25:06.171 --> 00:25:10.008
However, while we can't be sure of

00:25:10.008 --> 00:25:11.009
what's going to happen

00:25:11.009 --> 00:25:13.411
with Bitcoin's exchange rate,

00:25:13.411 --> 00:25:14.679
it's very volatile.

00:25:14.679 --> 00:25:15.780
Over a short, medium

00:25:15.780 --> 00:25:16.248
term time

00:25:16.248 --> 00:25:17.883
periods, could go up or down,

00:25:17.883 --> 00:25:18.850
whatever.

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:21.186
What we can be quite sure of

00:25:21.186 --> 00:25:24.489
is that the the central banks

00:25:24.489 --> 00:25:25.757
are going to continue

00:25:25.757 --> 00:25:28.293
printing more and more fiat.

00:25:28.293 --> 00:25:31.696
So the the way that I often sum

00:25:31.696 --> 00:25:34.366
that up is, you know, there's

00:25:34.366 --> 00:25:35.400
there's no ceiling

00:25:35.400 --> 00:25:36.601
on the Bitcoin price,

00:25:36.601 --> 00:25:38.370
but that's only because there's

00:25:38.370 --> 00:25:41.306
there's no floor on the fiat price

00:25:41.306 --> 00:25:42.474
because they're going to continue

00:25:42.474 --> 00:25:43.441
diluting it

00:25:43.441 --> 00:25:44.876
for years and generations.

00:25:44.876 --> 00:25:47.312
It's basically a guarantee that

00:25:47.312 --> 00:25:48.480
the central bankers themselves

00:25:48.480 --> 00:25:49.414
don't deny.

00:25:49.414 --> 00:25:50.682
Critics would say, though,

00:25:50.682 --> 00:25:52.517
if you're looking at it as a hedge,

00:25:53.585 --> 00:25:54.753
we're in a time of pretty

00:25:54.753 --> 00:25:56.087
rapid inflation in the US.

00:25:56.087 --> 00:25:57.088
And so why haven't

00:25:57.088 --> 00:26:00.091
we seen a huge uptick in adoption?

00:26:00.258 --> 00:26:00.992
How would you respond

00:26:00.992 --> 00:26:02.160
to those critics?

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.462
Well, where are we seeing adoption?

00:26:04.462 --> 00:26:05.764
I mean,

00:26:05.764 --> 00:26:06.665
I expect

00:26:06.665 --> 00:26:07.899
we're seeing more adoption

00:26:07.899 --> 00:26:08.733
in the countries

00:26:08.733 --> 00:26:10.302
that are seeing hyperinflation.

00:26:10.302 --> 00:26:10.769
Yeah.

00:26:10.769 --> 00:26:10.969
You know,

00:26:10.969 --> 00:26:11.937
America, of course,

00:26:11.937 --> 00:26:14.205
has seen an uptick in inflation,

00:26:14.205 --> 00:26:16.708
but it hasn't been anywhere near

00:26:16.708 --> 00:26:17.475
what some countries

00:26:17.475 --> 00:26:19.477
like Venezuela, for example,

00:26:19.477 --> 00:26:20.912
have seen.

00:26:20.912 --> 00:26:23.615
So, you know, there's it's a

00:26:23.615 --> 00:26:25.750
it's a multivariate problem

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:27.218
and it's difficult

00:26:27.218 --> 00:26:29.854
even for me to opine upon

00:26:29.854 --> 00:26:31.823
why certain people

00:26:31.823 --> 00:26:35.226
may adopt or not adopt Bitcoin.

00:26:35.293 --> 00:26:38.296
We are in a pretty privileged

00:26:38.330 --> 00:26:39.898
financial position.

00:26:39.898 --> 00:26:40.098
You know,

00:26:40.098 --> 00:26:41.366
basically if you have

00:26:41.366 --> 00:26:42.067
a credit card,

00:26:42.067 --> 00:26:43.268
if you have a bank account,

00:26:43.268 --> 00:26:45.070
you probably don't have

00:26:45.070 --> 00:26:46.471
a lot of incentives

00:26:46.471 --> 00:26:48.273
to look into hedges

00:26:48.273 --> 00:26:50.308
against hyperinflation.

00:26:50.308 --> 00:26:52.577
You know, even though,

00:26:52.577 --> 00:26:54.245
you know, the

00:26:54.245 --> 00:26:56.014
the dollar is going to continue

00:26:56.014 --> 00:26:56.881
to inflate,

00:26:57.882 --> 00:26:59.718
I think the hyperinflation

00:26:59.718 --> 00:27:00.619
risk of the dollar

00:27:00.619 --> 00:27:01.720
is still pretty low,

00:27:01.720 --> 00:27:02.821
at least relative

00:27:02.821 --> 00:27:04.623
to a lot of other countries.

00:27:04.623 --> 00:27:05.490
One thing we've noticed

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:07.125
so far walking around

00:27:07.125 --> 00:27:08.593
this conference has been

00:27:08.593 --> 00:27:10.895
everything from Free Ross to,

00:27:10.895 --> 00:27:12.030
you know, screw Dr.

00:27:12.030 --> 00:27:14.699
Fauci and RFK Junior type things.

00:27:14.699 --> 00:27:16.501
There's a cultural component

00:27:16.501 --> 00:27:17.902
that we're noticing here

00:27:17.902 --> 00:27:19.304
that seems pretty different

00:27:19.304 --> 00:27:21.940
than the Cypherpunk movement.

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:24.009
Your company has come under fire.

00:27:24.009 --> 00:27:25.510
You guys are in some ways,

00:27:25.510 --> 00:27:27.145
Ethereum proponents.

00:27:27.145 --> 00:27:28.213
What do you think of this

00:27:28.213 --> 00:27:29.381
sort of cultural strain

00:27:29.381 --> 00:27:30.915
that's present within some of this

00:27:30.915 --> 00:27:31.850
Bitcoin community?

00:27:31.850 --> 00:27:34.386
Yeah, you know, over the years,

00:27:34.386 --> 00:27:35.020
while I'm

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:38.023
very interested in the technology,

00:27:38.790 --> 00:27:39.224
I would say

00:27:39.224 --> 00:27:40.492
one of the more surprising things

00:27:40.492 --> 00:27:41.426
has been the

00:27:41.426 --> 00:27:43.161
sociological phenomenon

00:27:43.161 --> 00:27:45.096
and the interesting

00:27:45.096 --> 00:27:46.564
cultural divisions

00:27:46.564 --> 00:27:47.999
and strife that have happened,

00:27:47.999 --> 00:27:48.166
you know,

00:27:48.166 --> 00:27:49.668
for a variety of different reasons.

00:27:49.668 --> 00:27:51.302
But so, you know,

00:27:51.302 --> 00:27:52.871
Bitcoin started off

00:27:52.871 --> 00:27:54.305
and it really was

00:27:54.305 --> 00:27:55.740
the earliest adopters of Bitcoin,

00:27:55.740 --> 00:27:56.574
I would say

00:27:56.574 --> 00:27:57.876
were hardcore

00:27:57.876 --> 00:28:00.879
libertarians, anarcho capitalists.

00:28:00.945 --> 00:28:01.146
You know,

00:28:01.146 --> 00:28:04.049
they saw this as as freedom money,

00:28:04.049 --> 00:28:05.483
and it really was

00:28:05.483 --> 00:28:06.785
us against the world.

00:28:06.785 --> 00:28:07.252
As you know,

00:28:07.252 --> 00:28:08.887
we're developing

00:28:08.887 --> 00:28:11.523
a parallel economy, if you will,

00:28:11.523 --> 00:28:13.191
and it ties in

00:28:13.191 --> 00:28:14.492
with Cypherpunk movement

00:28:14.492 --> 00:28:15.960
from the sense that,

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:18.129
you know, cipher punks write code,

00:28:18.129 --> 00:28:19.597
they don't ask permission,

00:28:19.597 --> 00:28:20.732
they just build

00:28:20.732 --> 00:28:21.833
and they put it out there

00:28:21.833 --> 00:28:24.202
for the market to accept,

00:28:24.202 --> 00:28:26.971
digest, reject, what have you.

00:28:26.971 --> 00:28:28.273
Now,

00:28:28.273 --> 00:28:29.240
over the years,

00:28:29.240 --> 00:28:29.407
you know,

00:28:29.407 --> 00:28:31.476
Bitcoin has been

00:28:31.476 --> 00:28:32.677
attacked by a number

00:28:32.677 --> 00:28:34.245
of different people and entities

00:28:34.245 --> 00:28:36.481
in a number of different ways,

00:28:36.481 --> 00:28:38.583
especially as it has gone

00:28:38.583 --> 00:28:39.551
more mainstream

00:28:39.551 --> 00:28:40.385
and there have been boom

00:28:40.385 --> 00:28:41.186
and bust cycles

00:28:41.186 --> 00:28:42.654
and a lot of people have been hurt

00:28:42.654 --> 00:28:43.922
in various collapses.

00:28:43.922 --> 00:28:46.091
And we've seen various calls,

00:28:46.091 --> 00:28:48.393
you know, anti Bitcoin, anti

00:28:48.393 --> 00:28:50.028
the entire crypto space.

00:28:51.196 --> 00:28:52.731
This is, you know, it

00:28:52.731 --> 00:28:53.565
solidified

00:28:53.565 --> 00:28:54.899
even more of the sort of us

00:28:54.899 --> 00:28:56.768
against the rest of the world

00:28:56.768 --> 00:28:58.570
to some of these people.

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:01.473
And as the space has become larger

00:29:01.473 --> 00:29:02.941
and more diverse,

00:29:02.941 --> 00:29:03.975
we're actually seeing

00:29:03.975 --> 00:29:05.577
a fracturing

00:29:05.577 --> 00:29:08.279
even within the bitcoin,

00:29:08.279 --> 00:29:09.814
quote unquote community.

00:29:09.814 --> 00:29:10.081
You know,

00:29:10.081 --> 00:29:11.216
some people will get triggered

00:29:11.216 --> 00:29:12.817
if you even call it a community,

00:29:12.817 --> 00:29:14.085
because,

00:29:14.085 --> 00:29:14.319
you know,

00:29:14.319 --> 00:29:15.620
there is a saying

00:29:15.620 --> 00:29:18.289
which is Bitcoin is for enemies.

00:29:18.289 --> 00:29:18.923
This is

00:29:18.923 --> 00:29:20.091
really supposed to be

00:29:20.091 --> 00:29:23.027
a neutral technology and platform

00:29:23.027 --> 00:29:24.963
where anyone who follows

00:29:24.963 --> 00:29:26.498
the rules can use it

00:29:26.498 --> 00:29:27.365
regardless of

00:29:27.365 --> 00:29:28.933
if anyone else disagrees

00:29:28.933 --> 00:29:30.034
with who they are

00:29:30.034 --> 00:29:31.636
and what they're doing.

00:29:31.636 --> 00:29:34.539
So, you know, there are certain

00:29:34.539 --> 00:29:37.142
I would say, sub communities

00:29:37.142 --> 00:29:39.210
or subgroups of people

00:29:39.210 --> 00:29:41.679
that have coalesced over the years,

00:29:41.679 --> 00:29:44.048
some of them are more vitriolic

00:29:44.048 --> 00:29:45.049
than others.

00:29:45.049 --> 00:29:47.318
And, you know,

00:29:47.318 --> 00:29:47.986
they coalesce

00:29:47.986 --> 00:29:49.020
because they have certain

00:29:49.020 --> 00:29:50.388
shared principles,

00:29:50.388 --> 00:29:53.057
shared views of what is acceptable

00:29:53.057 --> 00:29:54.726
and what is unacceptable.

00:29:54.726 --> 00:29:56.761
And so that's some of the stuff

00:29:56.761 --> 00:29:57.662
that we have to deal with,

00:29:57.662 --> 00:29:59.831
especially on social media.

00:29:59.831 --> 00:30:02.400
There is-

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:03.601
it actually comes down

00:30:03.601 --> 00:30:05.436
to some phenomenon.

00:30:05.436 --> 00:30:06.738
There's a great article out there,

00:30:06.738 --> 00:30:08.506
I think it's called., it's

00:30:08.506 --> 00:30:10.575
like about knights and mooks.

00:30:10.575 --> 00:30:12.143
And basically

00:30:12.143 --> 00:30:12.844
it's similar

00:30:12.844 --> 00:30:14.813
to sort of political theater

00:30:14.813 --> 00:30:16.915
and phenomenon where,

00:30:16.915 --> 00:30:17.148
you know,

00:30:17.148 --> 00:30:18.416
you build up your narratives,

00:30:18.416 --> 00:30:19.651
you build your audience

00:30:19.651 --> 00:30:20.552
and you're following

00:30:20.552 --> 00:30:21.719
in your community.

00:30:21.719 --> 00:30:25.290
And one of the ways that you create

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:26.691
a more cohesive

00:30:26.691 --> 00:30:28.660
audience is by having enemies

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:29.460
that you attack

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:31.396
in certain ideological ways.

00:30:31.396 --> 00:30:32.430
And so that basically

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:33.331
that same thing

00:30:33.331 --> 00:30:35.233
is unfolded within Bitcoin.

00:30:35.233 --> 00:30:37.502
And and one of the results

00:30:37.502 --> 00:30:39.070
now is that,

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:39.971
you know, the quote

00:30:39.971 --> 00:30:42.907
unquote, Bitcoin maximalist

00:30:42.907 --> 00:30:43.474
is actually

00:30:43.474 --> 00:30:45.944
a very large, diverse tent.

00:30:45.944 --> 00:30:46.711
And there are

00:30:46.711 --> 00:30:48.112
there are different subgroups

00:30:48.112 --> 00:30:49.113
within that tent

00:30:49.113 --> 00:30:49.914
that have, you know,

00:30:49.914 --> 00:30:51.349
different shared beliefs.

00:30:51.349 --> 00:30:52.350
Is the culture around it

00:30:52.350 --> 00:30:53.318
a good or a bad thing?

00:30:53.318 --> 00:30:54.219
Your you're saying there's

00:30:54.219 --> 00:30:55.320
a lot of sort of circling

00:30:55.320 --> 00:30:55.887
the wagons.

00:30:55.887 --> 00:30:58.323
Does that lead to more people

00:30:58.323 --> 00:30:59.591
ultimately being interested?

00:30:59.591 --> 00:31:00.491
Is that a spectacle that

00:31:00.491 --> 00:31:01.593
people are drawn to?

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:05.330
Or is there some amount of

00:31:05.330 --> 00:31:05.864
you know,

00:31:05.864 --> 00:31:06.764
it's sort of straying

00:31:06.764 --> 00:31:08.066
from what people

00:31:08.066 --> 00:31:09.500
were initially attracted to?

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:09.734
Yeah.

00:31:09.734 --> 00:31:11.135
I mean, it's it's some of both.

00:31:11.135 --> 00:31:11.369
You know,

00:31:11.369 --> 00:31:12.136
there are certainly

00:31:12.136 --> 00:31:12.704
a lot of people

00:31:12.704 --> 00:31:15.306
who get turned off by

00:31:15.306 --> 00:31:16.941
some of the vitriol

00:31:16.941 --> 00:31:18.910
from some of the groups,

00:31:18.910 --> 00:31:21.212
whereas other people get interested

00:31:21.212 --> 00:31:23.548
and sucked into it.

00:31:23.548 --> 00:31:24.716
But if anything,

00:31:24.716 --> 00:31:27.085
I think it may be overblown.

00:31:27.085 --> 00:31:28.953
I think the real high level

00:31:28.953 --> 00:31:30.622
view of it

00:31:30.622 --> 00:31:33.658
is that even with all of the drama

00:31:33.658 --> 00:31:34.592
and strife and stuff

00:31:34.592 --> 00:31:36.828
that happens on various platforms

00:31:36.828 --> 00:31:38.963
in and around Bitcoin,

00:31:38.963 --> 00:31:40.598
the vast majority of people

00:31:40.598 --> 00:31:42.200
who use Bitcoin

00:31:42.200 --> 00:31:44.669
don't know about any of that.

00:31:44.669 --> 00:31:44.936
You know,

00:31:44.936 --> 00:31:45.770
they're not tuned

00:31:45.770 --> 00:31:47.372
into those social networks

00:31:47.372 --> 00:31:49.974
or at least those particular areas

00:31:49.974 --> 00:31:51.276
and communities

00:31:51.276 --> 00:31:52.176
where the drama

00:31:52.176 --> 00:31:53.544
and strife is happening.

00:31:53.544 --> 00:31:55.013
They just see Bitcoin

00:31:55.013 --> 00:31:56.748
as a thing that they're using.

00:31:56.748 --> 00:31:58.750
You were talking a little bit

00:31:58.750 --> 00:32:01.920
about how you really like the

00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:02.987
the bear market.

00:32:02.987 --> 00:32:04.188
The fact that

00:32:04.188 --> 00:32:05.590
there's a little bit less hype

00:32:05.590 --> 00:32:07.325
or the hype has been deflated

00:32:07.325 --> 00:32:09.360
surrounding crypto and Bitcoin,

00:32:09.360 --> 00:32:10.862
and we're seeing that a little bit

00:32:10.862 --> 00:32:11.596
at this conference.

00:32:11.596 --> 00:32:12.931
It's much more sparsely

00:32:12.931 --> 00:32:14.732
attended compared to past years.

00:32:14.732 --> 00:32:15.566
You were saying that you think

00:32:15.566 --> 00:32:16.067
this is actually

00:32:16.067 --> 00:32:17.502
a really good thing.

00:32:17.502 --> 00:32:18.202
Could you talk a little bit

00:32:18.202 --> 00:32:20.138
more about that? Absolutely.

00:32:21.139 --> 00:32:23.141
I'm not a fan of the

00:32:23.141 --> 00:32:24.742
the mania bull

00:32:24.742 --> 00:32:25.877
run phases

00:32:25.877 --> 00:32:27.712
because of the type of people

00:32:27.712 --> 00:32:31.683
that it attracts when the really-

00:32:31.683 --> 00:32:32.650
we might call them

00:32:32.650 --> 00:32:35.086
the tourists or the larpers

00:32:35.086 --> 00:32:37.055
or in many cases, the scammers,

00:32:37.055 --> 00:32:38.990
the con men show up.

00:32:38.990 --> 00:32:40.792
It's because they see, you

00:32:40.792 --> 00:32:42.860
know, fast, easy money

00:32:42.860 --> 00:32:45.396
and they're here to get rich quick.

00:32:45.396 --> 00:32:46.497
And, you know, I'm

00:32:46.497 --> 00:32:47.532
in this for the long term.

00:32:47.532 --> 00:32:49.100
I look at Bitcoin

00:32:49.100 --> 00:32:52.236
as our best opportunity

00:32:52.904 --> 00:32:54.272
to create

00:32:54.272 --> 00:32:57.542
a multigenerational project.

00:32:57.875 --> 00:32:58.343
You know,

00:32:58.343 --> 00:32:58.843
I think

00:32:58.843 --> 00:33:00.979
the average lifespan of a fiat

00:33:00.979 --> 00:33:03.614
currency is measured in decades.

00:33:03.614 --> 00:33:04.215
Bitcoin,

00:33:04.215 --> 00:33:05.883
I think we're coming up on

00:33:05.883 --> 00:33:08.086
14 years.

00:33:08.086 --> 00:33:10.221
So Bitcoin is actually

00:33:10.221 --> 00:33:11.422
getting close

00:33:11.422 --> 00:33:12.457
to exceeding

00:33:12.457 --> 00:33:13.558
the lifespan of the average

00:33:13.558 --> 00:33:14.625
fiat currency.

00:33:14.625 --> 00:33:15.960
And because it's an open

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:16.894
source project,

00:33:16.894 --> 00:33:18.029
I believe it is

00:33:18.029 --> 00:33:19.163
very well positioned

00:33:19.163 --> 00:33:20.565
to last for decades,

00:33:20.565 --> 00:33:21.432
if not centuries,

00:33:21.432 --> 00:33:23.101
as long as we take care of it

00:33:23.101 --> 00:33:24.035
and maintain it.

00:33:24.035 --> 00:33:25.336
But getting back to the,

00:33:25.336 --> 00:33:25.603
you know,

00:33:25.603 --> 00:33:26.871
building during bear

00:33:26.871 --> 00:33:28.272
markets, it's just

00:33:28.272 --> 00:33:29.474
it's a lot more peaceful

00:33:29.474 --> 00:33:30.641
and easy to do

00:33:30.641 --> 00:33:31.809
because you have

00:33:31.809 --> 00:33:33.211
fewer distractions.

00:33:33.211 --> 00:33:34.479
You know, as developers,

00:33:34.479 --> 00:33:36.647
we're able to put our heads down

00:33:36.647 --> 00:33:37.949
and, you know, focus

00:33:37.949 --> 00:33:40.184
on creating new functionality,

00:33:40.184 --> 00:33:41.552
delivering

00:33:41.552 --> 00:33:43.688
based on the demands of our clients

00:33:43.688 --> 00:33:44.989
and our users.

00:33:44.989 --> 00:33:45.990
And that's

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:47.225
what we've seen happening

00:33:47.225 --> 00:33:48.960
really even just this year,

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:50.028
like the past five or six

00:33:50.028 --> 00:33:51.329
months, it's been great

00:33:51.329 --> 00:33:53.031
from a developer standpoint.

00:33:53.031 --> 00:33:53.664
You know,

00:33:53.664 --> 00:33:54.198
even though

00:33:54.198 --> 00:33:55.199
I'm neutral on stuff

00:33:55.199 --> 00:33:57.835
like ordinals and tokens,

00:33:57.835 --> 00:33:59.637
it's just interesting to see

00:33:59.637 --> 00:34:01.372
that innovation happening.

00:34:01.372 --> 00:34:02.240
And yes, it

00:34:02.240 --> 00:34:04.709
you know, it creates some conflict,

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:06.744
but I like to see

00:34:06.744 --> 00:34:08.312
conflict and friction

00:34:08.312 --> 00:34:11.115
because that's how we come up with

00:34:11.115 --> 00:34:12.350
with new challenges,

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:13.785
which we then find

00:34:13.785 --> 00:34:14.919
new solutions to.

00:34:14.919 --> 00:34:15.553
Jameson Lopp,

00:34:15.553 --> 00:34:16.487
thank you for sitting down

00:34:16.487 --> 00:34:18.089
with Reason. Great to be here.

